1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,437
Under this ice, almost four kilometres down, there is a lake.

2
00:00:04,437 --> 00:00:08,003
It has been sealed in darkness for millions of years.

3
00:00:08,003 --> 00:00:11,233
And in twenty twelve, humans finally touched it.

4
00:00:11,699 --> 00:00:14,660
Some people say it hides a lost ecosystem.

5
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:19,313
Others whisper about secret bases, and creatures from another age.

6
00:00:19,313 --> 00:00:21,499
So what is actually down there?

7
00:00:21,499 --> 00:00:23,966
And what did the drill really find?

8
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:26,283
Tonight we keep a drill log.

9
00:00:26,283 --> 00:00:31,699
We start on the surface and go down, metre by metre, and every claim gets weighed.

10
00:00:32,166 --> 00:00:32,664
Surface.

11
00:00:32,664 --> 00:00:33,162
The ice.

12
00:00:33,162 --> 00:00:33,784
The drill.

13
00:00:33,784 --> 00:00:34,842
The breakthrough.

14
00:00:34,842 --> 00:00:35,402
The life.

15
00:00:35,402 --> 00:00:36,833
And finally, the myths.

16
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:37,766
Then a verdict.

17
00:00:38,233 --> 00:00:41,008
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

18
00:00:41,008 --> 00:00:41,933
Let's go down.

19
00:00:46,399 --> 00:00:47,271
Depth zero.

20
00:00:47,271 --> 00:00:48,222
The surface.

21
00:00:48,222 --> 00:00:56,066
The place is Vostok Station, a Russian base deep in East Antarctica, built in nineteen fifty-seven.

22
00:00:56,533 --> 00:00:59,333
It is one of the coldest places on Earth.

23
00:00:59,333 --> 00:01:09,033
The lowest air temperature ever measured at ground level was recorded here, about minus eighty-nine degrees Celsius, in nineteen eighty-three.

24
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:17,817
In the nineteen sixties, a Russian scientist, Andrei Kapitsa, looked at flight survey data and spotted something odd.

25
00:01:17,817 --> 00:01:20,234
Flat, smooth ground under the ice.

26
00:01:20,234 --> 00:01:22,366
He suggested there was a lake.

27
00:01:22,833 --> 00:01:27,180
Pilots had already noticed flat, mirror-like radar echoes.

28
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:28,979
The idea sounded absurd.

29
00:01:28,979 --> 00:01:33,251
Water, at four kilometres deep, in a land of minus fifty?

30
00:01:33,251 --> 00:01:35,500
Most scientists were doubtful.

31
00:01:35,966 --> 00:01:44,440
Then, in nineteen ninety-three, satellite radar altimetry showed a huge, perfectly flat patch on the ice surface above it.

32
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:47,983
That is how a floating ice sheet looks over a lake.

33
00:01:47,983 --> 00:01:49,233
The lake was real.

34
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:53,395
Pressure and the heat of the Earth keep the water liquid.

35
00:01:53,395 --> 00:01:59,166
Under that much ice, the melting point drops, and warmth rising from below does the rest.

36
00:01:59,633 --> 00:02:03,778
So we have a real station, a real lake, and a real puzzle.

37
00:02:03,778 --> 00:02:05,779
How big is it, and how deep?

38
00:02:05,779 --> 00:02:08,066
Next on the log, the ice itself.

39
00:02:08,533 --> 00:02:10,824
Depth, one kilometre and down.

40
00:02:10,824 --> 00:02:11,435
The ice.

41
00:02:11,435 --> 00:02:15,254
Every layer is a year of snow, pressed into glass.

42
00:02:15,254 --> 00:02:18,233
Inside it, tiny bubbles of ancient air.

43
00:02:18,699 --> 00:02:28,403
Scientists drilled one of the deepest ice cores ever made right here, and read the climate of hundreds of thousands of years in it.

44
00:02:28,403 --> 00:02:29,070
Ice ages.

45
00:02:29,070 --> 00:02:30,033
Warm periods.

46
00:02:30,033 --> 00:02:32,033
All recorded in frozen air.

47
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:36,297
The ice sheet above the lake is about four kilometres thick.

48
00:02:36,297 --> 00:02:43,133
The pressure at the bottom is roughly three hundred and fifty times the air pressure at your feet right now.

49
00:02:43,599 --> 00:02:49,088
The lake is long and narrow, about two hundred and fifty kilometres end to end.

50
00:02:49,088 --> 00:02:55,966
It is the largest of Antarctica's subglacial lakes, and among the biggest lakes on Earth by volume.

51
00:02:56,433 --> 00:02:57,907
And it is not alone.

52
00:02:57,907 --> 00:03:03,212
Radar has now found about four hundred lakes hiding under Antarctic ice.

53
00:03:03,212 --> 00:03:07,633
Vostok is simply the giant, and the first we really studied.

54
00:03:08,099 --> 00:03:13,037
The ice slowly slides over the lake, at roughly three metres a year.

55
00:03:13,037 --> 00:03:17,103
At the base, lake water freezes onto the ice from below.

56
00:03:17,103 --> 00:03:19,500
Scientists call it accretion ice.

57
00:03:19,966 --> 00:03:25,093
That frozen lake water carries tiny traces of what lives or floats below.

58
00:03:25,093 --> 00:03:31,133
It is a free sample, delivered from the dark, long before any drill touched the water.

59
00:03:31,599 --> 00:03:37,562
In nineteen ninety-nine, researchers examined that accretion ice and counted microbes.

60
00:03:37,562 --> 00:03:42,622
Thousands of cells per millilitre in one study, a few hundred in another.

61
00:03:42,622 --> 00:03:44,633
Small numbers, but real ones.

62
00:03:45,099 --> 00:03:48,071
Yet contamination is the great enemy here.

63
00:03:48,071 --> 00:03:52,600
A single stray cell from a glove or a drill can ruin everything.

64
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:57,200
That is why this story is full of caution, and full of arguments.

65
00:03:57,666 --> 00:03:59,817
So the ice already gives us hints.

66
00:03:59,817 --> 00:04:03,108
But to know the truth, someone had to go through it.

67
00:04:03,108 --> 00:04:04,500
Depth next: the drill.

68
00:04:04,966 --> 00:04:07,667
Depth, zero again, but now with a rig.

69
00:04:07,667 --> 00:04:14,633
Russian engineers began drilling here in nineteen ninety, through the borehole they called five G.

70
00:04:15,099 --> 00:04:20,580
They used a thermal drill, a hot head that melts ice, and electric cutting heads.

71
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:24,301
Each metre needed pulling the whole string up and down.

72
00:04:24,301 --> 00:04:26,466
It took years, with long pauses.

73
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:32,060
To keep the hole from closing under the pressure, they filled it with a liquid.

74
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:34,851
A kerosene-based fluid, mixed with a freon.

75
00:04:34,851 --> 00:04:37,966
Without it, the ice would squeeze the hole shut.

76
00:04:38,433 --> 00:04:40,505
That choice caused real alarm.

77
00:04:40,505 --> 00:04:44,651
A hole of oil and chemicals, heading toward a pristine lake.

78
00:04:44,651 --> 00:04:50,799
Environmental groups and scientists feared poisoning a world that had never been touched.

79
00:04:51,266 --> 00:04:54,806
Under the Antarctic Treaty, the plan faced scrutiny.

80
00:04:54,806 --> 00:05:02,500
Russia argued that the lake water would rise into the hole and push the fluid back, so the lake would stay clean.

81
00:05:02,966 --> 00:05:06,716
They also stopped well above the water, in earlier years.

82
00:05:06,716 --> 00:05:13,033
At about three thousand seven hundred metres they paused, to make sure no one cut through blind.

83
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:17,545
The work was only possible in the short Antarctic summer.

84
00:05:17,545 --> 00:05:23,792
In winter, the station is cut off, and temperatures drop so low that planes cannot land.

85
00:05:23,792 --> 00:05:25,566
Each season, a few weeks.

86
00:05:26,033 --> 00:05:30,822
Twenty-two years of effort, from the first hole to the last metres.

87
00:05:30,822 --> 00:05:31,466
Setbacks.

88
00:05:31,466 --> 00:05:32,467
A stuck drill.

89
00:05:32,467 --> 00:05:34,897
Funding gaps and political storms.

90
00:05:34,897 --> 00:05:37,899
Delay after delay, until the final season.

91
00:05:38,366 --> 00:05:42,762
By early twenty twelve the drill stood just metres above the water.

92
00:05:42,762 --> 00:05:44,862
The team knew what could happen.

93
00:05:44,862 --> 00:05:46,699
Nobody had done this before.

94
00:05:47,166 --> 00:05:48,401
Depth: almost there.

95
00:05:48,401 --> 00:05:50,500
Next on the log: the breakthrough.

96
00:05:50,966 --> 00:05:53,418
The fifth of February, twenty twelve.

97
00:05:53,418 --> 00:05:55,339
Late in the Antarctic summer.

98
00:05:55,339 --> 00:06:01,699
The crew watched their instruments in the control cabin as the drill went down the final metres.

99
00:06:02,166 --> 00:06:07,671
At three thousand seven hundred and sixty-nine metres, the drill broke through.

100
00:06:07,671 --> 00:06:11,991
Reports give about ten twenty-five in the evening, local time.

101
00:06:11,991 --> 00:06:14,500
Pressure dropped, and a surge began.

102
00:06:14,966 --> 00:06:18,201
Just as predicted, lake water rushed up the hole.

103
00:06:18,201 --> 00:06:25,000
It rose by tens of metres, pushing the drilling fluid ahead of it, and then it froze solid in the cold.

104
00:06:25,466 --> 00:06:26,625
There was relief.

105
00:06:26,625 --> 00:06:29,214
Valery Lukin and his team had done it.

106
00:06:29,214 --> 00:06:36,233
After more than twenty years, a human hole had reached a lake sealed for perhaps fifteen million years.

107
00:06:36,699 --> 00:06:39,074
But there was no water sample yet.

108
00:06:39,074 --> 00:06:41,729
The lake water had frozen in the hole.

109
00:06:41,729 --> 00:06:49,133
The team had to wait a full year, then drill again to cut out the frozen plug, in January twenty-thirteen.

110
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:53,884
That frozen lake water, a few metres of it, is what they brought up.

111
00:06:53,884 --> 00:06:59,933
It was mixed with the fluid, so the first job was to figure out what was lake, and what was not.

112
00:07:00,399 --> 00:07:11,866
They returned on the twenty-fifth of January, twenty fifteen, and entered the lake a second time, at about three thousand seven hundred and sixty-nine metres, with a sampling tool.

113
00:07:12,333 --> 00:07:15,968
The head of the expedition promised an honest account.

114
00:07:15,968 --> 00:07:18,190
The data would go to peer review.

115
00:07:18,190 --> 00:07:22,566
Not every claim would survive that process, and he admitted that.

116
00:07:23,033 --> 00:07:31,083
For the first time in history, humans were in contact with a lake that had been isolated for an unimaginable time.

117
00:07:31,083 --> 00:07:32,354
The hole was tiny.

118
00:07:32,354 --> 00:07:33,766
The meaning was not.

119
00:07:34,233 --> 00:07:35,579
The water was real.

120
00:07:35,579 --> 00:07:37,066
Now the big question.

121
00:07:37,066 --> 00:07:38,766
Is anything alive in it?

122
00:07:38,766 --> 00:07:39,899
Next on the log.

123
00:07:40,366 --> 00:07:44,269
What lives in a dark lake under four kilometres of ice?

124
00:07:44,269 --> 00:07:49,166
The answer, so far, is: very little, and nothing we can prove is new.

125
00:07:49,633 --> 00:07:54,618
In twenty twelve, Russian teams ran DNA tests on the drill-bit water.

126
00:07:54,618 --> 00:07:58,014
They found only about ten cells per millilitre.

127
00:07:58,014 --> 00:08:00,687
Very few, and four types of bacteria.

128
00:08:00,687 --> 00:08:04,300
Three of them came from the drilling fluid itself.

129
00:08:04,766 --> 00:08:07,255
So the first result was disappointing.

130
00:08:07,255 --> 00:08:08,631
Mostly contamination.

131
00:08:08,631 --> 00:08:16,033
That is science working properly: reporting what looks like life, and then showing it came in with the equipment.

132
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:18,980
Later work was more interesting.

133
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:29,678
A few years on, a research paper reported DNA of unknown groups, close to families of bacteria such as Burkholderia and Janthinobacterium.

134
00:08:29,678 --> 00:08:33,399
Real hints, but not proof of a Vostok ecosystem.

135
00:08:33,866 --> 00:08:34,849
Why not proof?

136
00:08:34,849 --> 00:08:39,270
Samples were tiny, mixed with fluid, and handled by many hands.

137
00:08:39,270 --> 00:08:42,077
Skeptics said it could all be stray DNA.

138
00:08:42,077 --> 00:08:45,866
The lake had not been shown to be a living world, yet.

139
00:08:46,333 --> 00:08:47,866
There is another clue.

140
00:08:47,866 --> 00:08:57,206
Studies of the ice suggest the lake could hold a lot of oxygen, released as air bubbles in melting ice slowly dissolve into the water.

141
00:08:57,206 --> 00:08:59,366
That is good news for any life.

142
00:08:59,833 --> 00:09:01,252
Extreme conditions.

143
00:09:01,252 --> 00:09:01,925
No light.

144
00:09:01,925 --> 00:09:03,867
Cold, just below freezing.

145
00:09:03,867 --> 00:09:04,913
Huge pressure.

146
00:09:04,913 --> 00:09:05,810
Little food.

147
00:09:05,810 --> 00:09:12,833
Yet life has been found in places almost like it, from deep ocean vents to other buried lakes.

148
00:09:13,299 --> 00:09:15,351
That is the real excitement.

149
00:09:15,351 --> 00:09:24,654
If life can live in a lake like this on Earth, it could live in ice-covered oceans on other worlds, like Jupiter's moon Europa.

150
00:09:24,654 --> 00:09:26,266
Vostok is a rehearsal.

151
00:09:26,733 --> 00:09:29,131
That is the honest alien angle.

152
00:09:29,131 --> 00:09:37,177
Not creatures in Antarctica, but a test run for how to explore hidden oceans without contaminating them.

153
00:09:37,177 --> 00:09:41,199
Space agencies study Vostok for exactly that reason.

154
00:09:41,666 --> 00:09:42,200
So life?

155
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:42,933
Maybe tiny.

156
00:09:42,933 --> 00:09:43,866
Maybe nothing.

157
00:09:43,866 --> 00:09:45,266
The evidence is thin.

158
00:09:45,266 --> 00:09:47,200
And then there are the myths.

159
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:48,266
Next on the log.

160
00:09:48,733 --> 00:09:49,924
Now the stories.

161
00:09:49,924 --> 00:09:51,190
Hidden Nazi base.

162
00:09:51,190 --> 00:09:52,158
Alien bunker.

163
00:09:52,158 --> 00:09:54,093
A lost world of dinosaurs.

164
00:09:54,093 --> 00:09:58,933
They all say the same thing: something is being hidden at Vostok.

165
00:09:59,399 --> 00:10:07,472
The Nazi base story grows from a real German expedition to Antarctica in nineteen thirty-eight and thirty-nine.

166
00:10:07,472 --> 00:10:09,436
It claimed a patch of land.

167
00:10:09,436 --> 00:10:13,800
It did not build a secret city under four kilometres of ice.

168
00:10:14,266 --> 00:10:20,021
The monster version says a creature lives in the lake, left from an earlier age.

169
00:10:20,021 --> 00:10:20,885
No evidence.

170
00:10:20,885 --> 00:10:26,999
A lake of near-freezing water, under that pressure, offers no food for large animals.

171
00:10:27,466 --> 00:10:31,731
The cover-up version says officials stopped science from looking.

172
00:10:31,731 --> 00:10:33,963
In reality, the opposite happened.

173
00:10:33,963 --> 00:10:40,000
Teams from several countries studied it, published papers, and argued in public for decades.

174
00:10:40,466 --> 00:10:41,984
And the alien story?

175
00:10:41,984 --> 00:10:48,509
It draws on the lake's strangeness and a few bad satellite pictures of ice formations.

176
00:10:48,509 --> 00:10:51,544
There is no craft, no signal, no object.

177
00:10:51,544 --> 00:10:54,200
Only a very remote scientific site.

178
00:10:54,666 --> 00:10:57,539
The truth is stranger in a quieter way.

179
00:10:57,539 --> 00:11:01,738
A hidden lake, real water, real pressure, almost no life.

180
00:11:01,738 --> 00:11:03,433
Time to score the lake.

181
00:11:03,899 --> 00:11:06,016
The drill is back on the surface.

182
00:11:06,016 --> 00:11:07,170
The log is closed.

183
00:11:07,170 --> 00:11:08,966
Let us look at what we have.

184
00:11:09,433 --> 00:11:10,037
Debrief.

185
00:11:10,037 --> 00:11:12,000
Time to score Lake Vostok.

186
00:11:12,466 --> 00:11:16,845
A huge liquid lake under four kilometres of ice: evidence.

187
00:11:16,845 --> 00:11:22,583
Reached by drilling in twenty twelve, and again in twenty fifteen: evidence.

188
00:11:22,583 --> 00:11:25,000
Oxygen-rich, dark, cold: likely.

189
00:11:25,466 --> 00:11:28,616
A thriving hidden ecosystem: unproven.

190
00:11:28,616 --> 00:11:31,434
A Nazi or alien base: no evidence.

191
00:11:31,434 --> 00:11:34,086
A monster in the lake: debunked.

192
00:11:34,086 --> 00:11:37,733
A cover-up: contradicted by the open record.

193
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:39,037
The verdict.

194
00:11:39,037 --> 00:11:42,038
The lake is real and the discovery is real.

195
00:11:42,038 --> 00:11:44,759
The aliens, bases and monsters are not.

196
00:11:44,759 --> 00:11:52,366
But the real question, whether anything lives there, is still open, and that is more thrilling than any myth.

197
00:11:52,833 --> 00:11:59,158
Somewhere under that ice, dark water is moving, slowly, in a place no light has ever reached.

198
00:11:59,158 --> 00:12:02,423
We keep hunting for strange worlds out in space.

199
00:12:02,423 --> 00:12:04,600
This one is right here on Earth.

200
00:12:05,066 --> 00:12:11,432
Next case: a lump of polished crystal, three thousand years old, found in an Assyrian palace.

201
00:12:11,432 --> 00:12:12,733
Was it a telescope?

202
00:12:13,200 --> 00:12:16,021
Do you think anything lives in Lake Vostok?

203
00:12:16,021 --> 00:12:21,533
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
